          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Telugu for a mother's elder sister, then the Telugu for a niece."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: వియ్యంకుడు",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "వియ్యంకుడు — viyyaṅkuḍu — \"a co-father-in-law; one's child's father-in-law\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "పెళ్ళి తరువాత నాన్న, వియ్యంకుడు మంచి స్నేహితులు అయ్యారు. peḷḷi taruvāta nānna, viyyaṅkuḍu mañci snēhitulu ayyāru. After the wedding, dad and his co-father-in-law became good friends."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The fathers of a married couple are each other's వియ్యంకుడు (viyyaṅkuḍu); the mothers are వియ్యపురాలు. వియ్యం is the bond between the two families."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "వియ్యంకుడు (viyyaṅkuḍu) is listed in DEDR's appendix of Indo-Aryan loans (DEDR App. 58) beside viyyamu, \"a marriage alliance\"; the family goes back to Sanskrit vivāha, \"marriage\" (CDIAL 11920)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
